    "value": "In Vert.x 5, the API for reactive PostgreSQL client connections has evolved compared to earlier versions. The previous usage of `PgPool.client` has been replaced by a new builder-based approach using `PgBuilder.client()` for creating a `SqlClient` (which works with pooled connections). Here’s an example for Vert.x 5:\n\n```java\nPgConnectOptions connectOptions = new PgConnectOptions()\n  .setPort(5432)\n  .setHost(\"the-host\")\n  .setDatabase(\"the-db\")\n  .setUser(\"user\")\n  .setPassword(\"secret\");\n\n// Pool options\nPoolOptions poolOptions = new PoolOptions().setMaxSize(5);\n\n// Create the client pool (use .using(vertx) if you want to pass your Vertx instance)\nSqlClient client = PgBuilder\n  .client()\n  .with(poolOptions)\n  .connectingTo(connectOptions)\n  .build();\n```\n\nThe previous `PgPool.client()` style is **deprecated/removed** in Vert.x 5. You now use the builder pattern: `PgBuilder.client().with(...).connectingTo(...).build()`. If you want to explicitly use a Vert.x instance, add `.using(vertx)` in the chain.\n\nFor full upgrade guidance or more code snippets, see the official Vert.x 5 documentation and migration guides【3:0†source】【3:1†source】.",
