    "value": "Here is what you need to know about `CorsHandler.create()` and `addOrigin` in Vert.x 5 (or similar recent versions):\n\n## `CorsHandler.create()`\n- In Vert.x, `CorsHandler.create()` is used to instantiate a CORS handler for your routes.\n- It can optionally take a pattern string to whitelist origins, e.g., `CorsHandler.create(\"https://mydomain.com\")`. In practice, you'll often see it with `\"*\"` to allow all origins (use with caution)【3:0†source】.\n\n### Example:\n```java\nrouter.route().handler(\n    CorsHandler.create(\"*\") // or .create(\"https://example.com\")\n    .allowedMethod(HttpMethod.GET)\n    .allowedMethod(HttpMethod.POST)\n    .allowedHeader(\"Content-Type\")\n    .allowedHeader(\"Authorization\")\n);\n```\n\n## `addOrigin`\n- Starting in Vert.x 5, `CorsHandler` introduces an `addOrigin(String origin)` method which allows you to specify multiple origins in code, rather than passing a RegExp in `.create()`【3:2†source】【3:8†source】【3:9†source】.\n- This method is especially useful if you want to allow a list of explicit origin domains.\n\n### Example:\n```java\nrouter.route().handler(\n    CorsHandler.create()\n        .addOrigin(\"https://foo.example.com\")\n        .addOrigin(\"https://bar.example.com\")\n        .allowedMethod(HttpMethod.GET)\n        .allowedMethod(HttpMethod.POST)\n        .allowedHeader(\"Content-Type\")\n);\n```\n\n**Summary of usage:**\n- `CorsHandler.create()` can be used alone for wildcard or pattern-based origins.\n- Use `addOrigin` (or `addOrigins` for multiple domains) for explicit whitelist control in code.\n\n**Notes:**\n- If credentials are needed, do **not** use `\"*\"`; explicitly list your allowed domains instead, and also call `.allowCredentials(true)`.\n- Allowing all origins (`\"*\"`) is not safe for production if your API sends any sensitive data or allows credentials.\n\n**References:**  \n- Vert.x documentation and sample code【3:0†source】【3:1†source】【3:2†source】【3:8†source】.",
